body:
The way I ended up solving this, based on the example plugin.

I saw that adding `$this` to a file when a file is require()d within a class's method interferes when analyzing the remainder of that method.

I also added a beforeAnalyzeFile hook to TysonAndre/psalm - that seems like it should be generally useful

```php
<?php
namespace Psalm\Examples\Template;

use Psalm\Internal\Analyzer\FileAnalyzer;
use Psalm\Plugin\Hook\BeforeAnalyzeFileInterface;
use Psalm\Type;

class HTMLTemplateAnalyzer implements BeforeAnalyzeFileInterface
{
    // HTMLTemplateDynamic is a subclass of HTMLTemplate adding __get() and __set() so that Psalm won't warn about undeclared properties
    const VIEW_CLASS = 'HTMLTemplateDynamic';

    /**
     * Set the type of $this to HTMLTemplate3Dynamic in files in my-app/templates
     * @override
     */
    public static function beforeAnalyzeFile(FileAnalyzer $file_analyzer)
    {
        if (!preg_match('@my-app/templates/@', $file_analyzer->getFilePath())) {
            return;
        }
        $context = $file_analyzer->context;
        if (isset($context->vars_in_scope['$this'])) {
            // require() is called from another global scope or method scope?
            return;
        }
        $context->vars_in_scope['$this'] = new Type\Union([
            new Type\Atomic\TNamedObject(self::VIEW_CLASS),
        ]);
    }
}
```

The usage at Vimeo is a little weird:

We have controllers and views. Controllers have methods that assign `$this->foo = "bar";`, and then the controller does

```php
foreach ($this as $key => $value) {
    $view_args[$key] = $value;
}
```

Then those args are extracted into the current scope with `extract` so `$this->foo` becomes `$foo` in the template itself, and `$this` in the template belongs to a separate class with view-related methods.

This pattern existed many years before Psalm, so I had to accommodate it. This `TemplateChecker` contains some of the necessary logic - [it figures out the controller method from an annotation within each file](https://github.com/vimeo/psalm/blob/ce03be2dc085b66c05ef2ef0c82badaeac0b2df4/examples/TemplateChecker.php#L33), and then [figures out what controller properties are set](https://github.com/vimeo/psalm/blob/ce03be2dc085b66c05ef2ef0c82badaeac0b2df4/examples/TemplateChecker.php#L99) when the method is called. It does this by simulating a method call, also simulating any calls on `$this` within that method (that's the expensive part, as there can sometimes be a lot of them).
